  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ There exists $n\\in N$ such that $2n$ is not composite.",
  "worked_solution": "If we denote\n\n$$\nP(n):2n\\text{ is composite},\n$$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∃n\\in N,\\,¬P(n).\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∃n\\in N,\\,¬P(n)) & ≡∀n\\in N,\\,¬¬P(n) \\\\  & ≡∀n\\in N,\\,P(n).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $∀n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **For all** $n\\in N,$ $2n$ is composite.",
